# Caddyfile — TLS terminator in front of vulos-relayd.
#
# Caddy obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates automatically and proxies
# HTTPS (443) + HTTP (80) to the relay's loopback plain-HTTP port (127.0.0.1:8443).
# WebSocket upgrades pass through reverse_proxy transparently — no extra config.
#
# Install:  sudo install -Dm644 deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#           sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PATH MODE (recommended to start) — single hostname, HTTP-01 challenge, stock
# Caddy, no DNS-provider plugin. Set VULOS_RELAY_PATH_MODE=1 in relayd.env.
# Reach a box at https://relay.example.com/t/box1/
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
relay.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8443
}

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUBDOMAIN MODE (nicer URLs: https://box1.relay.example.com) — needs a WILDCARD
# certificate, which Let's Encrypt only issues over the DNS-01 challenge. That
# requires a Caddy built with your DNS provider's plugin (e.g. via xcaddy:
#   xcaddy build --with github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare
# or the caddy Docker image with the plugin baked in) and an API token.
#
# Unset VULOS_RELAY_PATH_MODE, add wildcard DNS (see docs/CLOUD-SETUP.md), then
# replace the block above with this one (Cloudflare shown; swap for your DNS provider):
#
# *.relay.example.com, relay.example.com {
#	tls {
#		dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
#	}
#	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8443
# }
